{#
    The invoice's screens, in one place.

    Expects: invoice, `current` naming which tab is being viewed ('view', 'print', 'packing-slip'
    or 'payments'), and `printable` — true only on a page that IS the printed document, where
    "Print" means this page rather than a link to it.

    One partial rather than the same <nav> copied onto four templates, which is how the order side
    ended up with four near-identical copies of its own tab bar (detail, form, log and the invoice
    document each grew one).

    The <nav> itself is `admin/_partials/sales_document_action_bar.html.twig` now, shared with the
    order's and the quote's. What stays here is everything that is the INVOICE's: the four screens
    and two documents raised against it, which of them a draft withholds, and the narrow window in
    which it may be edited at all.

    `pdf: null` below: NOT a standalone Invoice PDF tab (dropped in the left-alignment pass) — the
    PDF/Download disclosure's own "Print Invoice"/"Download Invoice" already reach the identical
    `admin_invoice_print` route, and a bar does not need the same PDF reachable from two buttons,
    the same reasoning `admin/order/_tab_nav.html.twig` already applies. Quote is unaffected in the
    same direction: its own standalone tab is gone too, for the identical reason.
#}
{% include 'admin/_partials/sales_document_action_bar.html.twig' with {
    document: invoice,
    idPrefix: 'invoice',
    documentNoun: 'invoice',
    ariaLabel: 'Invoice detail actions',
    navClass: 'no-print',
    view: {href: path('admin_invoice_detail', {id: invoice.id}), current: current == 'view'},
    edit: (invoice.canEditOnStatus() and not document_is_locked(invoice))
        ? {href: path('admin_invoice_edit', {id: invoice.id}), label: 'Edit'}
        : null,
    pdf: null,
    barTemplate: 'admin/invoice/_bar_actions.html.twig',
    barContext: {current: current},
    printMenuTemplate: 'admin/invoice/_print_menu.html.twig',
    printContext: {current: current, printable: printable|default(false)},
    cloneUrl: path('admin_invoice_clone', {id: invoice.id}),
    lockUrl: path('admin_invoice_lock', {id: invoice.id}),
    unlockUrl: path('admin_invoice_unlock', {id: invoice.id}),
    overflowTemplate: 'admin/invoice/_overflow_actions.html.twig',
    overflowContext: {availableActions: availableActions|default([])},
} only %}
{#
    `admin_invoice_edit` exists now — order-linked or not, an invoice may be corrected the same way
    it is created, refused only on `Invoice::canEditOnStatus()` (Completed, Cancelled — raise a
    credit memo instead) or a manual freeze. `canEditOnStatus()` is the one and only authoritative
    "can this be edited" gate; nothing else here re-derives that answer. This mirrors
    `InvoiceController::edit()`'s own guards exactly, which is deliberate: the tab and the route
    must refuse on the same facts, or the tab would offer a link the route immediately bounces, or
    the route would accept a save the tab never offered a way to reach.

    A Completed or Cancelled invoice stays without an Edit tab: it changes through documents raised
    against it instead — a payment, a credit note, a sales return — because by then it is a record
    of something that already happened (Completed) or is permanent by construction (Cancelled),
    not a working document this screen still owns.

    It is also why the shared bar takes `edit` as a nullable VALUE rather than a boolean: a document
    that may be edited right now hands it a href, and one that may not hands it nothing.
#}
